    "evidence_text": "Neutrophil extracellular traps enriched in oxidized mitochondrial DNA are interferogenic and contribute to lupus-like disease\n\nMitochondrial ROS are also necessary for spontaneous NETosis of low-density granulocytes from individuals with systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\nMitochondrial ROS inhibition in vivo reduces disease severity and type I IFN responses in a mouse model of lupus.\n\nAmyloid-DNA Composites of Bacterial Biofilms Stimulate Autoimmunity.\n\nGenome-wide association studies have recently identified at least 15 susceptibility loci for systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\n\nBASOPHILS AND THE T HELPER 2 ENVIRONMENT CAN PROMOTE THE DEVELOPMENT OF LUPUS NEPHRITIS\n\nBasophils were also present in the lymph nodes and spleen of subjects with SLE.\n\nNeutrophil extracellular chromatin traps connect innate immune response to autoimmunity\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is a multisystem autoimmune disease with complicated genetic inheritance.\n\nThe PD-1 expression levels of SLE patients were significantly increased compared with those of the healthy controls.\n\nNetting neutrophils are major inducers of type I IFN production in pediatric systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is a systemic autoimmune disease characterized by a breakdown of tolerance to nuclear antigens and the development of immune complexes.\n\nAlthough neutrophils have long been shown to be associated with lupus, their potential role in disease pathogenesis remains elusive.\n\nThe role of neutrophils in the pathogenesis of systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\nProteins and enzymes externalized by lupus NETs can affect vascular health by inducing endothelial apoptosis and oxidizing lipoproteins.\n\nFuture studies should focus on how to selectively target these immunostimulatory pathways in this disease.\n\nEosinophil pathogenicity mechanisms and therapeutics in neuromyelitis optica.\n\nIn the presence of complement, eosinophils greatly increased cell killing by a complement-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (CDCC) mechanism.\n\nLesion severity was increased in transgenic hypereosinophilic mice.\n\nAbstract A woman, now 59 has been followed for 13 years with several manifestations of Behcet's disease.\n\nIn 1970 a poorly differentiated diffuse lymphocytic lymphoma appeared and was treated with radiotherapy.\n\nThe subsequent course of her lymphoproliferative disorder suggest that it was in fact benign.",
